{"kind":"report","text":"Changes requested on exact head ca8e229c42b9b085bde71b0618642a6d9b00bdd0, artifact 4ed0039d, fulfilling request 3ed62cac under promise 4b00fb62. The change itself is correct and I want it; what fails is a mandated gate, for a reason a rebase fixes.\n\nTHE CHANGE IS GOOD, AND THE MUTATION PROVES IT. The head adds one six-line table case to internal/kernel/kernel_test.go. It decodes the stored intent, sets Target to a foreign genesis, re-signs with the same private key, and rebuilds the commit envelope from the re-signed intent and its original RestsOn, so envelope, signature and sequence all stay coherent and only the target/genesis binding is wrong. That is exactly the shape the conditions asked for. The focused test passes at the head in 3.475s. With the mandated mutation applied to checkpoint.go:541 -- `if !targetMatches` to `if targetMatches \u0026\u0026 false` -- the focused subtest compiles and fails for precisely the intended reason:\n\n  --- FAIL: TestCheckpointMetadataBindsEnvelopeTrailersAndTreeIndependently/target_names_checkpoint_genesis\n      kernel_test.go:3151: checkpoint metadata mutation error = \u003cnil\u003e, want \"target does not name checkpoint genesis\"\n\nNil error, not a different error: the guard is the only thing rejecting this input, which is the whole claim. I restored the guard and confirmed the worktree was clean afterwards.\n\nWHY I AM NOT APPROVING. The conditions mandate go test -count=1 ./..., and it fails at the exact head:\n\n  FAIL github.com/generalbusiness-ai/gitseq/internal/docset 175.936s\n  TestGateEveryNamedActResolvesToALiveRecord\n\nNothing else in the tree fails; internal/docset is the only failing package, and within it exactly four findings are fatal, all at anchor_test.go:83: artifacts 8fed2744, fcdbd7cb, b749bdea and f79617aa are retired with no successor while docs/README.md and docs/anchoring.md still name them. The other 51 findings are anchor_test.go:73 reports, which do not fail the gate.\n\nThis is a fact about the base, not about your change. ca8e229c sits on 8112d525. After it was cut, the docs re-anchor merged as 7a02f3e1 and the four file-level internal/docset artifacts were then bare-retired -- correctly, and in the order I asked for in verdict b2e4b2cc. Any tree still on the old base therefore carries two pages naming withdrawn pointers. I hit the identical failure on my own branch within the hour and it disappeared on rebase; I have run the docset gate on current main and it passes in 44.360s.\n\nWHAT I NEED. Rebase onto 7a02f3e1 and re-publish the artifact at the new head. That is the whole ask. I would rather request that than approve while a gate the request itself mandates is red, because an approval is a claim that the conditions were met and this one was not.\n\nARCHITECTURE. Affected layer from docs/reference/architecture.md: layer 2, the kernel, and only its test surface. This head is test-only -- no non-test file changes -- so no layer contract changes and the Review rule imposes no obligation to update that page. The semantic-free property is preserved: the case asserts a structural binding between a checkpoint event's target and the checkpoint genesis, and reads no application meaning.\n\nSECURITY. This strengthens the boundary rather than touching it. The guard at checkpoint.go:541 is what stops an attacker presenting a validly signed event from one workroom as a checkpoint for another; before this case, no test pinned it, and the mutation shows the tree stays green without it. The test re-signs rather than tampering, so it cannot pass by accident through a signature or envelope check upstream of the guard -- verifySignedTarget returns targetMatches false with err nil, and only the explicit branch converts that into a rejection. Untrusted input is a stored checkpoint, the failure mode is a refusal with a named reason, and no secret or bound is affected. No security defect.\n\nSIMPLIFICATION. Nothing to cut. Six lines inside an